{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on journal-floor draft1 at exact03e4a13a66103dfa7518272145553bcc2d5beafe, Source8083c3c0, requestab483d9e. Complete note read in full; native Source exact/current/directly bound to governing promis ebb6edf4a/requestf9fcee34 and actual prepare assigned. Documentation only; these are design counterexamples, not observed failures of a floor implementation. All findings together:\n\nF1 [P1], note114-116/129: Apply the10000-entry margin before the4096-step clamp. The highest floor allowed by the rules is at most currentFloor+4096. Subtract10000 after choosing that maximum yields F\u003c=currentFloor-5904, so every proposed floor fails floor-back, even when the archive is far ahead. The actual F3 policy can never advance. Choose the desired retained-tail margin first, then clamp an advancing target to step/receipt/segment/recent constraints. Add a witness using the actual10000margin and4096step together, not only the1000-entry test-host bound.\n\nF2 [P1], note108-131: Give floor admission the recorded evidence it needs. FloorInput contains floor/commit/through; floor-receipt checks only that through names a local entry, not that commit is the confirmed archive for this incarnation. floor-segment needs the first position of a Git segment, which neither that input nor the journal/state records. Current ArchiveRows.confirm is explicitly non-history metadata (archive-row.ts:1-3,70-72,134-140). Replay cannot apply the claimed same journal/state-only predicates from these fields. Specify the canonical recorded confirmation/boundary data and its trust boundary with the host's remote readback, how it binds commit/scope/incarnation/through, and what native/replay each validate. This need not pretend offline replay proves external durability, but must not silently consult mutable provider/bookkeeping state. Include unconfirmed/unrelated commit and inconsistent segment-boundary refusals.\n\nF3 [P1], note257-261/291-293: Preserve the lifetime idempotency binding or explicitly adopt a versioned change. An OLD exact intent expires; a newly signed DIFFERENT intent using the same actor/key with fresh notAfter does not. Removing the index and accepted state lets that new intent pass where judge.ts:215-219 requires idempotency-mismatch for the scope's lifetime, before expiry. This can repeat an effect under a reused key. The900-second rule does not protect against fresh reuse. Specify bounded tombstone/receipt semantics or an explicit adopted epoch/version change, including consequences for capacity and clients. Witness old exact retry, conflicting fresh reuse, restart and replay.\n\nF4 [P2], note79-89: Specify the incremental archive index/reader contract, including S2b preservation. If pass1 retainsA and pass2's manifest lists only newB while the snapshot tree keepsA, current readJournalArchive rejectsA as outside its manifest (journal-reader.ts:85-95). head/manifest also reject absolute through\u003e=4096 (journal-manifest.ts:33-41). A per-pass cutoff change alone does not make the stated10000-entry clone readable. Define cumulative versus delta manifests, how older retained-input and foreign-source pin/fact/completeness metadata remain verifiable, reader/parent/rebuild behavior and their bounded work; retain the now-landed S2b guarantees. Parent commits do not by themselves supply a missing cumulative index in the current read contract. The witness must read and replay the later cumulative clone beyond4096, not just produce several commits.\n\nF5 [P2], note275-284: A blanket start-at-F loses evidence that the design deliberately keeps. Concrete lane example: a recent local signer can still read their old kept item-opening entry via Reads.#mine (:139-143), but a retained input named only by that entry becomes forbidden if #carried scans onlyF..head (:186-191). priorDetachedBounds is also historical evidence, not just a traversal cursor: handlers.ts:164-168 uses successful earlier checks to resolve ambiguous redacted delivery bindings. Starting atF can discard that evidence while the owed binding still needs it. LocalSigners additionally validates its cutoff and next-1 anchors (:121-127); latest signer entries alone do not define that control's floor/backfill transition. Define bounded scans over kept entries plus the tail, any retained proof summaries/causal roots, and index/anchor migration, with the same replay rules and preserved authorization. Add kept-old-entry retained reads and redacted pending-work/index restart cases.\n\nF6 [P2], note143-157: Reclaim entries leaving the previous kept set. Example: floor1keeps42 belowF100; its operation later closes and another signer entry supersedes it; floor2 moves to200 and excludes42. Deleting only the interval from oldF100 to200 never deletes42, but the new kept count omits it. Physical rows and accepted-intent state diverge from the stated count/replay. Specify old-kept minus new-kept reclamation and its per-turn work bound (which is not covered by floor-step alone), and retain the exact kept membership needed across restart/state snapshots. Add a two-floor close-and-reclaim witness.\n\nF7 [P2], note198-200: Preserve non-entry byte charges. SqliteStore.logicalBytes(:183-199) sums domain-deduplicated entry, retained_input AND retained_value bytes. It is not an entry-only counter. The draft says logicalBytes sums held entries only while retaining all retained inputs, which would omit real storage/reservation charges. Only actually deleted entry contributions should disappear; retained-input/value charges and deduplication remain. Witness nontrivial retained data and byte-capacity reservations across a floor.\n\nF8 [P2], note223-225/303-306: Define receipt lookup and stale-clone/live-tail recovery over multiple floors. The scope frame stores only latestF/receipt/count, but archived(n) is required to return the receipt of the floor entry that removedn; later floors can prune that floor entry too. No durable receipt-range lookup is specified. Separately, floor-ahead boundsF against the floor's receipt, not an arbitrary reader clone: cloneThrough1000 and laterF9000 makes the proposed service continuation1001 fall BELOWF. Choose a bounded retained receipt scheme or a validated current covering receipt, and define how a client obtains/validates a newer archive or reports incomplete when the floor moves before its tail read. Add two-floor/restart/old-clone and concurrent floor-advance witnesses.\n\nThe direction addresses the actual head.seq capacity problem and correctly separates an archive copy from reclaiming capacity. The confirmed-receipt requirement, recent window, direct current-state/open-work retention, unchanged partition/site absolute-position limits, and explicit test-host/hosted slices are useful constraints. They do not close the counterexamples above. No pruning, bound change, production admission or deployment is approved by this draft.\n\nValidation: only this note changes against its parent; git diff --check0. All57structured cited code locations resolved and inspected against the exact candidate, with abbreviated nearby locations checked in context; runtime effects.ts was disambiguated from its validation helper. The underlying code is source evidence, not a floor implementation. Governing archive note sections15.3-15.6, source readers/indices/judges/accounting and existing replay context were read. No package install, gate/suite/native/project execution, shared-source/ref edit, push or deployment.\n\nOwn read-only evidence /private/tmp/checker-floor-d1-p0e9cds_ retains exact note/head, cited source snapshots, reference map and static findings. All evidence/copies freeze permanently after closure; read or NEWstable-copy only, never execute/edit originals. Collector ambiguity/key handling and unmatched shell search globs were corrected as preparation issues without changing source.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8083c3c0